{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "scriptId": "pl-03-total-cost-overheads-wastage-stock",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Total Cost, Overheads, Wastage and Unsold Stock",
    "description": "Include repairs, transport, installation, damaged goods, fixed overheads and unsold stock before judging the real profit or loss.",
    "teachingApproach": "Start by exposing hidden costs, then show why rejected or unsold units still belong to the outlay. Build toward target revenue, per-saleable-unit pricing, fixed-overhead break-even and partial-stock targets using one consistent total-outlay model.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 12, Profit and Loss. Approved scope: Teach that real cost includes all amounts spent to make goods sale-ready: purchase, repairs, transport, octroi, installation and fixed overheads when the question includes them. Cover broken/rejected/unsold units, useful output after wastage, per-unit target selling price, and simple break-even with fixed cost plus variable cost. Distinguish sunk cost of all units from revenue earned only on saleable units. Include quality-rejection and production examples shaped like the objective exercise. Learners should total the outlay first, then decide the required total revenue, then divide by units actually sold."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn total cost, overheads, wastage and unsold stock.\n\nA scooter is bought for ₹4700 and needs ₹800 of repairs before it can be sold. If we ignore the repair bill, the profit will look bigger than it really is.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The sticker price is not always the whole cost. If you buy a scooter and pay for repairs before selling it, those repairs are part of the money invested.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Purchase = ₹4700\nRepairs = ₹800\nTrue C.P. = ₹5500\n\nIf sold for ₹5800, profit is ₹300 — not ₹1100.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
